A||||12||drednot||Clutch / CV shaft question ???||||11||
Z||000000||drednot||08-18-2001||08:19 PM||||So I am finally getting around to changing my clutch. <P>My plan of attack is to remove my driver side suspension, remove the CV shaft, scoot the transaxle over while supporting the engine so I can get to the clutch.<P>I have just about got the suspension out, but the only problem I am having now, is my CV shaft won't come out!!<P>Hanes says to use a big screwdriver to pull it out of the transaxle.  no.  that is not working. <P>suggestions?  comments?  What do you all think about this method of changing the clutch?<BR><p>[This message has been edited by drednot (edited 08-18-2001).]||||reg||11
Z||000001||LS1swap||08-18-2001||09:04 PM||||yes just pry with a large screwdriver or lug wrench. you my need to use a small piece of 2X4 to pry against. you are dropping the engine cradle, yes? and you will need to support the engine from above.||||reg||1
Z||000002||85Josh||08-18-2001||09:31 PM||||drednot, I just finished bolting mine back together from doing this. The axles can be a b***h, just keep prying. The procedure from Ed Parks website is what I used, here's a link: <BR> <A HREF="http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html#top" TARGET=_blank>http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html#top</A> <BR>Good Luck!<P>------------------<BR>85 2M4(Lil' Bit)||||reg||1
Z||000003||drednot||08-18-2001||09:32 PM||||No, I am not dropping the engine cradle.  just mowing the trans over so I can get to the clutch.  this will work, right??||||reg||1
Z||000004||LS1swap||08-19-2001||08:18 AM||||I don't know I have always dropped the cradle to do it, I am not going to say it is simple but neither will doing it without removing the cradle. Removing the cradle will allow you to take the trans. All the way out and give you a lot more room to work. To do this you would have to disconnect the exhaust at the  y pipe (the bolts with springs) remove the four tans/cradle bolts there are a few ground wires to remove if memory serves me right. The two bolts (one each side) on the lower control arm ball joints and the four cradle bolts Front engine mount and stabilizer ( small shock) and the dust flap.( you can leave the rest of the exhaust together). Don't get me wrong I am not telling you it can't be done without removing the cradle it is just I have never done it that way. It is very tight down there and I am not sure you will have enough room with out removing the cradle. Just another helpful hint remember to disconnect the coolant pipe that is hooked to the trans, it is slotted so you will not have to take the nut all the way off just loosen it and pull up. Also if you do decide to remove the trans just remove the slave at the studs don't open the system unless you have to, and there are two wires to unplug the VSS and one other ( can't remember what that was) I am going from memory here maybe someone else can point you out to some online instructions. Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Indiana_resto_guy||08-19-2001||10:34 AM||||You can change the clutch this way, but you will need to remove the 2 rear cradle bolts and allow the cradle to swing down a bit (6 to 8 inches). To let this happen the dog bone will need disconnected on 1 end.<BR>Your plan of attack sounds good, for more details try searching in theogers cave. There is lots of useful information to be found there.<BR>As soon as you complete your search, come on back and ask more ?s if you have any!<BR>The half shafts can be very tight, just find a good solid pry point.||||reg||1
Z||000006||drednot||08-19-2001||11:55 AM||||I just got the suspension out.  I never did get the axle shaft out right, I just took off the boot and pulled it apart.  worked ok.  Now I guess I have to take out my 2 cradle bolts and dogbone next.  I will keep everyone updated.  thanks for the info.<p>[This message has been edited by drednot (edited 08-19-2001).]||||reg||11
Z||000007||drednot||08-20-2001||03:59 PM||||For the CV shafts, I just cut them off at the boot and pulled them apart.  They just would not come out of the transmission.  I had a pry bar in-between and I was hitting it with a sledge hammer, and they would not budge.   Only problem is that some parts fell out of the one shaft.  about 50 little metal rods fell out and 2 big circular things fell out too.  The reason for changing the clutch is because It would not fully dis-engage.  I got the clutch out and I am trying to figure out what the problem was.  I thought It was a bad bearing, but it spun fine.  what else could it be?||||reg||11
Z||000008||drednot||08-20-2001||04:27 PM||||bearings-<P>Does a Fiero have a throughout bearing (Spelling) between the friction disk and the flywheel?  if so, mine does not have one.<P> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Indiana_resto_guy||08-20-2001||04:35 PM||||The order is from the engine:<P>Clutch disk<BR>Pressure Plate (friction)<BR>Throw-out Bearing (mounts in a fork inside the)<BR>Transmission<P>Sorry to say, you just wasted your CV joints unless you know how to clean them put them back together and reinstall a one piece boot (on the inside only) and have the proper tools to put it back together.<BR>There is a split boot available but it is for the outside only to be used in place of the one piece style.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000010||drednot||08-20-2001||06:30 PM||||but I thought it had a T/O bearing in the flywheel and a RELEASE BEARING in the transmission on the clutch lever?!?||||reg||1
Z||000011||Steve Normington||08-20-2001||06:43 PM||||There was no T/O bearing in my 2.8 4 spd.<P>I don't know if you checked this first, but 95% of clutch problems are usually the clutch slave/lever/fork problems. How much travel was your clutch slave getting?||||reg||1
Z||000012||LS1swap||08-20-2001||07:01 PM||||I think you may be confusing the throw out bearing and a pilot bearing. the throw out bearing is what pushes on the springs of the pressure plat a pilot bearing goes in the crank and supports the input shaft on most trans. the 2.8 doesn't have one. The input shaft on the trans. has bearings front and bach and there for doesn't need one. hope this helps.||||reg||1
